# Optional example: driving one-shot mode from cron instead of running
# --loop as a daemon (deploy/mercados.service).
#
# BEST-EFFORT, UNSUITABLE FOR SUB-5-MINUTE WINDOWS: cron's minimum
# granularity is one minute, it does not guarantee your command starts
# exactly on the minute (system load, other jobs, and cron's own polling
# interval all add jitter), and it will not compensate if a run overlaps
# the next one. The three messages' pre-notice thresholds go as low as
# 2 minutes (Section 1.B) — a scheduler with multi-second jitter eats
# directly into that margin. If you need reliable sub-minute freshness,
# use deploy/mercados.service (--loop) instead, not this file.
#
# Install (as the 'mercados' service user):
#   crontab -e
#   # then paste the line below, adjusted for your install path and
#   # your Python 3.14 interpreter (see README.md for install methods)

* * * * * cd /opt/mercados && /opt/mercados/.venv/bin/python3.14 main.py --incident-interval 60 >> /opt/mercados/data/cron.log 2>&1

# Notes:
#   - No --loop flag: each invocation is a single one-shot render/publish
#     pass, matching main.py's default mode.
#   - --incident-interval is accepted but irrelevant here: one-shot mode
#     runs a single incident-detection pass inline per invocation rather
#     than starting the background polling task at all.
#   - flock(1) can prevent overlapping runs if a single pass ever takes
#     longer than your cron interval:
#       * * * * * flock -n /tmp/mercados.lock -c 'cd /opt/mercados && /opt/mercados/.venv/bin/python3.14 main.py' >> /opt/mercados/data/cron.log 2>&1
